1
00:00:04,358 --> 00:00:09,718
It was the first time that a grave had opened in my road of life, and the gap it made in the

2
00:00:09,718 --> 00:00:16,480
smooth ground was wonderful. The figure of my sister in her chair by the kitchen fire,

3
00:00:16,480 --> 00:00:23,877
haunted me night and day. That the place could possibly be, without her, was something my mind

4
00:00:23,879 --> 00:00:29,839
seemed unable to compass; and whereas she had seldom or never been in my thoughts of late,

5
00:00:29,839 --> 00:00:35,116
I had now the strangest ideas that she was coming towards me in the street, or that she would

6
00:00:35,116 --> 00:00:41,758
presently knock at the door. In my rooms too, with which she had never been at all associated,

7
00:00:41,758 --> 00:00:46,519
there was at once the blankness of death and a perpetual suggestion of the sound of her

8
00:00:46,519 --> 00:00:53,480
voice or the turn of her face or figure, as if she were still alive and had been often there.

9
00:00:53,480 --> 00:00:58,078
Whatever my fortunes might have been, I could scarcely have recalled my sister with much

10
00:00:58,078 --> 00:01:05,319
tenderness. But I suppose there is a shock of regret which may exist without much tenderness.

11
00:01:05,319 --> 00:01:10,758
Under its influence (and perhaps to make up for the want of the softer feeling) I was seized with

12
00:01:10,758 --> 00:01:16,839
a violent indignation against the assailant from whom she had suffered so much; and I felt that on

13
00:01:16,839 --> 00:01:25,078
sufficient proof I could have revengefully pursued Orlick, or any one else, to the last extremity.

14
00:01:25,078 --> 00:01:30,038
Having written to Joe, to offer him consolation, and to assure him that I would come to the

15
00:01:30,038 --> 00:01:36,839
funeral, I passed the intermediate days in the curious state of mind I have glanced at. I went

16
00:01:36,839 --> 00:01:43,078
down early in the morning, and alighted at the Blue Boar in good time to walk over to the forge.

17
00:01:43,078 --> 00:01:47,398
It was fine summer weather again, and, as I walked along, the times when I was

18
00:01:47,398 --> 00:01:54,038
a little helpless creature, and my sister did not spare me, vividly returned. But they

19
00:01:54,038 --> 00:02:00,116
returned with a gentle tone upon them that softened even the edge of Tickler. For now,

20
00:02:00,116 --> 00:02:05,839
the very breath of the beans and clover whispered to my heart that the day must come when it would

21
00:02:05,839 --> 00:02:12,800
be well for my memory that others walking in the sunshine should be softened as they thought of me.

22
00:02:12,800 --> 00:02:19,237
At last I came within sight of the house, and saw that Trabb and Co. had put in a funereal

23
00:02:19,237 --> 00:02:27,038
execution and taken possession. Two dismally absurd persons, each ostentatiously exhibiting

24
00:02:27,038 --> 00:02:32,038
a crutch done up in a black bandage,—as if that instrument could possibly communicate

25
00:02:32,038 --> 00:02:38,961
any comfort to anybody,—were posted at the front door; and in one of them I recognised a postboy

26
00:02:38,961 --> 00:02:45,078
discharged from the Boar for turning a young couple into a sawpit on their bridal morning,

27
00:02:45,078 --> 00:02:49,358
in consequence of intoxication rendering it necessary for him to ride his horse

28
00:02:49,358 --> 00:02:56,038
clasped round the neck with both arms. All the children of the village, and most of the women,

29
00:02:56,038 --> 00:03:01,718
were admiring these sable warders and the closed windows of the house and forge;

30
00:03:01,718 --> 00:03:07,038
and as I came up, one of the two warders (the postboy) knocked at the door,—implying

31
00:03:07,038 --> 00:03:12,917
that I was far too much exhausted by grief to have strength remaining to knock for myself.

32
00:03:12,917 --> 00:03:19,398
Another sable warder (a carpenter, who had once eaten two geese for a wager) opened the door,

33
00:03:19,398 --> 00:03:26,000
and showed me into the best parlour. Here, Mr. Trabb had taken unto himself the best table,

34
00:03:26,000 --> 00:03:31,641
and had got all the leaves up, and was holding a kind of black Bazaar, with the aid of a quantity

35
00:03:31,641 --> 00:03:38,961
of black pins. At the moment of my arrival, he had just finished putting somebody’s hat into black

36
00:03:38,961 --> 00:03:47,559
long-clothes, like an African baby; so he held out his hand for mine. But I, misled by the action,

37
00:03:47,559 --> 00:03:54,559
and confused by the occasion, shook hands with him with every testimony of warm affection.

38
00:03:54,559 --> 00:04:00,319
Poor dear Joe, entangled in a little black cloak tied in a large bow under his chin,

39
00:04:00,319 --> 00:04:06,398
was seated apart at the upper end of the room; where, as chief mourner, he had evidently been

40
00:04:06,398 --> 00:04:15,756
stationed by Trabb. When I bent down and said to him, “Dear Joe, how are you?” he said, “Pip, old

41
00:04:15,758 --> 00:04:23,839
chap, you knowed her when she were a fine figure of a—” and clasped my hand and said no more.

42
00:04:23,839 --> 00:04:29,078
Biddy, looking very neat and modest in her black dress, went quietly here and there,

43
00:04:29,078 --> 00:04:35,519
and was very helpful. When I had spoken to Biddy, as I thought it not a time for talking I went and

44
00:04:35,519 --> 00:04:43,277
sat down near Joe, and there began to wonder in what part of the house it—she—my sister—was.

45
00:04:43,277 --> 00:04:48,398
The air of the parlour being faint with the smell of sweet-cake, I looked about for the table of

46
00:04:48,398 --> 00:04:53,398
refreshments; it was scarcely visible until one had got accustomed to the gloom, but there was a

47
00:04:53,398 --> 00:04:58,800
cut-up plum cake upon it, and there were cut-up oranges, and sandwiches, and biscuits, and two

48
00:04:58,800 --> 00:05:04,641
decanters that I knew very well as ornaments, but had never seen used in all my life; one full of

49
00:05:04,641 --> 00:05:11,358
port, and one of sherry. Standing at this table, I became conscious of the servile Pumblechook in

50
00:05:11,358 --> 00:05:17,237
a black cloak and several yards of hatband, who was alternately stuffing himself, and making

51
00:05:17,237 --> 00:05:24,038
obsequious movements to catch my attention. The moment he succeeded, he came over to me (breathing

52
00:05:24,038 --> 00:05:32,839
sherry and crumbs), and said in a subdued voice, “May I, dear sir?” and did. I then descried

53
00:05:32,839 --> 00:05:39,641
Mr. and Mrs. Hubble; the last-named in a decent speechless paroxysm in a corner. We were all going

54
00:05:39,641 --> 00:05:46,277
to “follow,” and were all in course of being tied up separately (by Trabb) into ridiculous bundles.

55
00:05:46,277 --> 00:05:52,678
“Which I meantersay, Pip,” Joe whispered me, as we were being what Mr. Trabb called “formed” in

56
00:05:52,680 --> 00:06:01,277
the parlour, two and two,—and it was dreadfully like a preparation for some grim kind of dance;

57
00:06:01,277 --> 00:06:07,038
“which I meantersay, sir, as I would in preference have carried her to the church myself, along with

58
00:06:07,038 --> 00:06:12,398
three or four friendly ones wot come to it with willing harts and arms, but it were considered wot

59
00:06:12,398 --> 00:06:18,917
the neighbours would look down on such and would be of opinions as it were wanting in respect.”

60
00:06:18,917 --> 00:06:22,839
“Pocket-handkerchiefs out, all!” cried Mr. Trabb at this point,

61
00:06:22,839 --> 00:06:29,438
in a depressed business-like voice. “Pocket-handkerchiefs out! We are ready!”

62
00:06:29,438 --> 00:06:34,078
So we all put our pocket-handkerchiefs to our faces, as if our noses were bleeding,

63
00:06:34,078 --> 00:06:41,398
and filed out two and two; Joe and I; Biddy and Pumblechook; Mr. and Mrs. Hubble. The remains

64
00:06:41,398 --> 00:06:45,678
of my poor sister had been brought round by the kitchen door, and, it being a point of

65
00:06:45,680 --> 00:06:50,758
Undertaking ceremony that the six bearers must be stifled and blinded under a horrible black velvet

66
00:06:50,758 --> 00:06:56,237
housing with a white border, the whole looked like a blind monster with twelve human legs,

67
00:06:56,237 --> 00:07:04,116
shuffling and blundering along, under the guidance of two keepers,—the postboy and his comrade.

68
00:07:04,116 --> 00:07:08,557
The neighbourhood, however, highly approved of these arrangements, and we were much admired

69
00:07:08,559 --> 00:07:13,000
as we went through the village; the more youthful and vigorous part of the community

70
00:07:13,000 --> 00:07:18,038
making dashes now and then to cut us off, and lying in wait to intercept us at points

71
00:07:18,038 --> 00:07:23,800
of vantage. At such times the more exuberant among them called out in an excited manner

72
00:07:23,800 --> 00:07:30,641
on our emergence round some corner of expectancy, “Here they come!” “Here they are!” and we were all

73
00:07:30,641 --> 00:07:37,559
but cheered. In this progress I was much annoyed by the abject Pumblechook, who, being behind me,

74
00:07:37,559 --> 00:07:43,160
persisted all the way as a delicate attention in arranging my streaming hatband, and smoothing my

75
00:07:43,160 --> 00:07:49,557
cloak. My thoughts were further distracted by the excessive pride of Mr. and Mrs. Hubble,

76
00:07:49,559 --> 00:07:56,398
who were surpassingly conceited and vainglorious in being members of so distinguished a procession.

77
00:07:56,398 --> 00:08:01,800
And now the range of marshes lay clear before us, with the sails of the ships on the river growing

78
00:08:01,800 --> 00:08:07,398
out of it; and we went into the churchyard, close to the graves of my unknown parents,

79
00:08:07,398 --> 00:08:15,038
Philip Pirrip, late of this parish, and Also Georgiana, Wife of the Above. And there,

80
00:08:15,038 --> 00:08:20,000
my sister was laid quietly in the earth, while the larks sang high above it,

81
00:08:20,000 --> 00:08:25,680
and the light wind strewed it with beautiful shadows of clouds and trees.

82
00:08:25,680 --> 00:08:30,078
Of the conduct of the worldly minded Pumblechook while this was doing,

83
00:08:30,078 --> 00:08:36,038
I desire to say no more than it was all addressed to me; and that even when those noble passages

84
00:08:36,038 --> 00:08:42,718
were read which remind humanity how it brought nothing into the world and can take nothing out,

85
00:08:42,718 --> 00:08:47,319
and how it fleeth like a shadow and never continueth long in one stay,

86
00:08:47,319 --> 00:08:51,839
I heard him cough a reservation of the case of a young gentleman who came unexpectedly into

87
00:08:51,839 --> 00:08:57,199
large property. When we got back, he had the hardihood to tell me that he wished my sister

88
00:08:57,199 --> 00:09:01,718
could have known I had done her so much honour, and to hint that she would have considered it

89
00:09:01,718 --> 00:09:08,519
reasonably purchased at the price of her death. After that, he drank all the rest of the sherry,

90
00:09:08,519 --> 00:09:14,319
and Mr. Hubble drank the port, and the two talked (which I have since observed to be customary in

91
00:09:14,319 --> 00:09:20,078
such cases) as if they were of quite another race from the deceased, and were notoriously

92
00:09:20,078 --> 00:09:28,116
immortal. Finally, he went away with Mr. and Mrs. Hubble,—to make an evening of it, I felt sure,

93
00:09:28,116 --> 00:09:34,597
and to tell the Jolly Bargemen that he was the founder of my fortunes and my earliest benefactor.

94
00:09:34,597 --> 00:09:41,597
When they were all gone, and when Trabb and his men—but not his Boy; I looked for him—had crammed

95
00:09:41,597 --> 00:09:49,038
their mummery into bags, and were gone too, the house felt wholesomer. Soon afterwards, Biddy,

96
00:09:49,038 --> 00:09:55,917
Joe, and I, had a cold dinner together; but we dined in the best parlour, not in the old kitchen,

97
00:09:55,917 --> 00:10:01,000
and Joe was so exceedingly particular what he did with his knife and fork and the saltcellar and

98
00:10:01,000 --> 00:10:07,358
what not, that there was great restraint upon us. But after dinner, when I made him take his pipe,

99
00:10:07,358 --> 00:10:12,277
and when I had loitered with him about the forge, and when we sat down together on the great block

100
00:10:12,277 --> 00:10:19,597
of stone outside it, we got on better. I noticed that after the funeral Joe changed his clothes so

101
00:10:19,597 --> 00:10:25,160
far, as to make a compromise between his Sunday dress and working dress; in which the dear fellow

102
00:10:25,160 --> 00:10:30,519
looked natural, and like the Man he was. He was very much pleased by my asking if

103
00:10:30,519 --> 00:10:35,398
I might sleep in my own little room, and I was pleased too; for I felt that I had done

104
00:10:35,398 --> 00:10:40,800
rather a great thing in making the request. When the shadows of evening were closing in,

105
00:10:40,800 --> 00:10:45,597
I took an opportunity of getting into the garden with Biddy for a little talk.

106
00:10:45,597 --> 00:10:50,917
“Biddy,” said I, “I think you might have written to me about these sad matters.”

107
00:10:50,917 --> 00:10:57,078
“Do you, Mr. Pip?” said Biddy. “I should have written if I had thought that.”

108
00:10:57,078 --> 00:10:59,879
“Don’t suppose that I mean to be unkind, Biddy,

109
00:10:59,879 --> 00:11:03,319
when I say I consider that you ought to have thought that.”

110
00:11:03,319 --> 00:11:05,319
“Do you, Mr. Pip?”

111
00:11:05,319 --> 00:11:11,237
She was so quiet, and had such an orderly, good, and pretty way with her,

112
00:11:11,237 --> 00:11:17,038
that I did not like the thought of making her cry again. After looking a little at

113
00:11:17,038 --> 00:11:22,038
her downcast eyes as she walked beside me, I gave up that point.

114
00:11:22,038 --> 00:11:26,917
“I suppose it will be difficult for you to remain here now, Biddy dear?”

115
00:11:26,917 --> 00:11:33,961
“Oh! I can’t do so, Mr. Pip,” said Biddy, in a tone of regret but still of quiet conviction.

116
00:11:33,961 --> 00:11:39,199
“I have been speaking to Mrs. Hubble, and I am going to her to-morrow. I hope

117
00:11:39,199 --> 00:11:44,917
we shall be able to take some care of Mr. Gargery, together, until he settles down.”

118
00:11:44,917 --> 00:11:48,438
“How are you going to live, Biddy? If you want any mo—”

119
00:11:48,438 --> 00:11:55,000
“How am I going to live?” repeated Biddy, striking in, with a momentary flush upon her face. “I’ll

120
00:11:55,000 --> 00:11:59,800
tell you, Mr. Pip. I am going to try to get the place of mistress in the new school nearly

121
00:11:59,800 --> 00:12:04,961
finished here. I can be well recommended by all the neighbours, and I hope I can be industrious

122
00:12:04,961 --> 00:12:10,839
and patient, and teach myself while I teach others. You know, Mr. Pip,” pursued Biddy,

123
00:12:10,839 --> 00:12:15,758
with a smile, as she raised her eyes to my face, “the new schools are not like the old,

124
00:12:15,758 --> 00:12:21,839
but I learnt a good deal from you after that time, and have had time since then to improve.”

125
00:12:21,839 --> 00:12:25,839
“I think you would always improve, Biddy, under any circumstances.”

126
00:12:25,839 --> 00:12:30,519
“Ah! Except in my bad side of human nature,” murmured Biddy.

127
00:12:30,519 --> 00:12:37,116
It was not so much a reproach as an irresistible thinking aloud. Well! I thought I would give

128
00:12:37,116 --> 00:12:45,398
up that point too. So, I walked a little further with Biddy, looking silently at her downcast eyes.

129
00:12:45,398 --> 00:12:49,678
“I have not heard the particulars of my sister’s death, Biddy.”

130
00:12:49,680 --> 00:12:55,116
“They are very slight, poor thing. She had been in one of her bad states—though they had

131
00:12:55,116 --> 00:13:01,277
got better of late, rather than worse—for four days, when she came out of it in the evening,

132
00:13:01,277 --> 00:13:08,718
just at tea-time, and said quite plainly, ‘Joe.’ As she had never said any word for a long while,

133
00:13:08,718 --> 00:13:14,398
I ran and fetched in Mr. Gargery from the forge. She made signs to me that she wanted him to sit

134
00:13:14,398 --> 00:13:19,961
down close to her, and wanted me to put her arms round his neck. So I put them round his neck,

135
00:13:19,961 --> 00:13:25,680
and she laid her head down on his shoulder quite content and satisfied. And so she presently said

136
00:13:25,680 --> 00:13:34,917
‘Joe’ again, and once ‘Pardon,’ and once ‘Pip.’ And so she never lifted her head up any more,

137
00:13:34,917 --> 00:13:41,237
and it was just an hour later when we laid it down on her own bed, because we found she was gone.”

138
00:13:41,237 --> 00:13:44,961
Biddy cried; the darkening garden, and the lane,

139
00:13:44,961 --> 00:13:49,559
and the stars that were coming out, were blurred in my own sight.

140
00:13:49,559 --> 00:13:52,358
“Nothing was ever discovered, Biddy?”

141
00:13:52,358 --> 00:13:54,277
“Nothing.”

142
00:13:54,277 --> 00:13:57,038
“Do you know what is become of Orlick?”

143
00:13:57,038 --> 00:14:01,436
“I should think from the colour of his clothes that he is working in the quarries.”

144
00:14:01,438 --> 00:14:07,038
“Of course you have seen him then?—Why are you looking at that dark tree in the lane?”

145
00:14:07,038 --> 00:14:10,559
“I saw him there, on the night she died.”

146
00:14:10,559 --> 00:14:12,161
“That was not the last time either, Biddy?”

147
00:14:13,277 --> 00:14:19,961
“No; I have seen him there, since we have been walking here.—It is of no use,” said Biddy,

148
00:14:19,961 --> 00:14:24,199
laying her hand upon my arm, as I was for running out,

149
00:14:24,199 --> 00:14:30,358
“you know I would not deceive you; he was not there a minute, and he is gone.”

150
00:14:30,358 --> 00:14:35,917
It revived my utmost indignation to find that she was still pursued by this fellow,

151
00:14:35,917 --> 00:14:41,641
and I felt inveterate against him. I told her so, and told her that I would spend any money or take

152
00:14:41,641 --> 00:14:47,917
any pains to drive him out of that country. By degrees she led me into more temperate talk,

153
00:14:47,917 --> 00:14:53,680
and she told me how Joe loved me, and how Joe never complained of anything,—she didn’t say,

154
00:14:53,680 --> 00:15:00,438
of me; she had no need; I knew what she meant,—but ever did his duty in his way of life, with a

155
00:15:00,438 --> 00:15:07,237
strong hand, a quiet tongue, and a gentle heart. “Indeed, it would be hard to say too much for

156
00:15:07,237 --> 00:15:13,160
him,” said I; “and Biddy, we must often speak of these things, for of course I shall be often down

157
00:15:13,160 --> 00:15:19,480
here now. I am not going to leave poor Joe alone.” Biddy said never a single word.

158
00:15:19,480 --> 00:15:22,000
“Biddy, don’t you hear me?”

159
00:15:22,000 --> 00:15:23,559
“Yes, Mr. Pip.”

160
00:15:23,559 --> 00:15:26,839
“Not to mention your calling me Mr. Pip,—which appears to

161
00:15:26,839 --> 00:15:30,641
me to be in bad taste, Biddy,—what do you mean?”

162
00:15:30,641 --> 00:15:34,078
“What do I mean?” asked Biddy, timidly.

163
00:15:34,078 --> 00:15:37,557
“Biddy,” said I, in a virtuously self-asserting manner,

164
00:15:37,559 --> 00:15:40,641
“I must request to know what you mean by this?”

165
00:15:40,641 --> 00:15:42,480
“By this?” said Biddy.

166
00:15:42,480 --> 00:15:46,959
“Now, don’t echo,” I retorted. “You used not to echo, Biddy.”

167
00:15:46,961 --> 00:15:51,879
“Used not!” said Biddy. “O Mr. Pip! Used!”

168
00:15:51,879 --> 00:15:55,597
Well! I rather thought I would give up that point too. After

169
00:15:55,597 --> 00:16:00,559
another silent turn in the garden, I fell back on the main position.

170
00:16:00,559 --> 00:16:06,358
“Biddy,” said I, “I made a remark respecting my coming down here often, to see Joe,

171
00:16:06,358 --> 00:16:12,038
which you received with a marked silence. Have the goodness, Biddy, to tell me why.”

172
00:16:12,597 --> 00:16:17,199
“Are you quite sure, then, that you WILL come to see him often?” asked Biddy,

173
00:16:17,199 --> 00:16:23,480
stopping in the narrow garden walk, and looking at me under the stars with a clear and honest eye.

174
00:16:23,480 --> 00:16:27,319
“O dear me!” said I, as if I found myself compelled to give up Biddy

175
00:16:27,319 --> 00:16:32,597
in despair. “This really is a very bad side of human nature! Don’t say any more,

176
00:16:32,597 --> 00:16:36,680
if you please, Biddy. This shocks me very much.”

177
00:16:36,680 --> 00:16:40,718
For which cogent reason I kept Biddy at a distance during supper,

178
00:16:40,718 --> 00:16:45,758
and when I went up to my own old little room, took as stately a leave of her as I could,

179
00:16:45,758 --> 00:16:52,641
in my murmuring soul, deem reconcilable with the churchyard and the event of the day. As

180
00:16:52,641 --> 00:16:57,038
often as I was restless in the night, and that was every quarter of an hour,

181
00:16:57,038 --> 00:17:03,839
I reflected what an unkindness, what an injury, what an injustice, Biddy had done me.

182
00:17:03,839 --> 00:17:09,597
Early in the morning I was to go. Early in the morning I was out, and looking in,

183
00:17:09,597 --> 00:17:15,879
unseen, at one of the wooden windows of the forge. There I stood, for minutes,

184
00:17:15,879 --> 00:17:21,680
looking at Joe, already at work with a glow of health and strength upon his face that

185
00:17:21,680 --> 00:17:27,000
made it show as if the bright sun of the life in store for him were shining on it.

186
00:17:27,000 --> 00:17:31,078
“Good-bye, dear Joe!—No, don’t wipe it off—for God’s sake,

187
00:17:31,078 --> 00:17:36,277
give me your blackened hand!—I shall be down soon and often.”

188
00:17:36,277 --> 00:17:40,557
“Never too soon, sir,” said Joe, “and never too often, Pip!”

189
00:17:40,559 --> 00:17:45,438
Biddy was waiting for me at the kitchen door, with a mug of new milk and a crust of bread.

190
00:17:45,438 --> 00:17:51,038
“Biddy,” said I, when I gave her my hand at parting, “I am not angry, but I am hurt.”

191
00:17:51,038 --> 00:17:54,319
“No, don’t be hurt,” she pleaded quite pathetically;

192
00:17:54,319 --> 00:17:58,078
“let only me be hurt, if I have been ungenerous.”

193
00:17:58,078 --> 00:18:05,038
Once more, the mists were rising as I walked away. If they disclosed to me, as I suspect they did,

194
00:18:05,038 --> 00:18:10,199
that I should not come back, and that Biddy was quite right, all I can say is,—they were

195
00:18:10,199 --> 00:18:12,000
quite right too. Chapter 36.
